<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<pretext xmlns:ns0="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <ns0:include href="../docinfo.ptx" />
  <article>
    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25in" xml:id="ws-volume-strategy">
      <title>Strategies and Practice for Volume</title>

      <objectives>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Decide when a solid requires the general slicing method rather than a formula for a
              solid of revolution.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Choose between the washer/disk method and the shell method for a solid of revolution.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Set up and evaluate volume integrals using slicing, washers, and shells.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </objectives>

      <introduction>
        <title>Summary of volume-finding strategies</title>

        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p> If the solid is <em>not</em> a solid of revolution, the only way to find its
                volume is by <alert>general slicing</alert>: cut the solid into cross-sections
                perpendicular to some axis, express the cross-sectional area <m>A(x)</m> as a
                function of position, and integrate <m>V=\int_a^b A(x)\,dx</m>. Choose slices that
                make the cross-sections as simple — and as similar to each other — as possible. </p>

              <p> For example, if a solid is formed by two cylinders of the same radius whose axes
                meet at right angles, slicing perpendicular to <em>both</em> axes at once gives
                square cross-sections, a much better choice than trying to slice parallel to only
                one axis. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p> If the solid <em>is</em> a solid of revolution, the basic decision is <alert>washers</alert>
                (or disks) versus <alert>shells</alert>. First decide which way you want to
                integrate over the region — this may depend on whether the region is vertically
                simple or horizontally simple, or your affinity for integrating inverse functions.
                Then: </p>

              <p>
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p> If the direction of integration is <alert>parallel</alert> to the axis of
                      rotation, use washers/disks. </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p> If the direction of integration is <alert>perpendicular</alert> to the axis
                      of rotation, use shells. </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>

              <p> Usually both methods are possible — there are exceptions to this rule of thumb,
                but often one method is much easier than the other. </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p> Once you've chosen a method, remember: </p>

              <p>
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p> For washers, the <alert>outer</alert> and <alert>inner</alert> radii are
                      the distances from the axis of rotation to the far and near boundaries of the
                      region. </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p> For shells, the <alert>radius</alert> is the distance from the axis of
                      rotation to the shell, and the <alert>height</alert> is the length of the
                      region at that position (top minus bottom, or right minus left). </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <page>
        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p> A hump of <m>y=\sin x</m>, <m>0 \leq x \leq \pi</m>, is revolved around the <m>x</m>-axis.
              Which method would you use to find the volume, and which variable would you
              integrate against? (You don't need to compute the volume.) </p>
          </statement>

          <hint>
            <p> The region is naturally bounded above by <m>y=\sin x</m> and below by <m>y=0</m>,
              so the natural direction of integration is <m>dx</m>. Compare this direction to the
              axis of rotation. </p>
          </hint>

          </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p> The region bounded by <m>x=2-y^2</m> and <m>y=x</m> is revolved around the line <m>
                x=-3</m>. Which method would you use, and which variable would you integrate
              against? </p>
          </statement>

          <hint>
            <p> The curve <m>x=2-y^2</m> is naturally a function of <m>y</m>, and the axis of
              rotation <m>x=-3</m> is a vertical line. </p>
          </hint>

          </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p> The region between <m>y=(2-x)(4-x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis is revolved around the <m>
                y</m>-axis. Which method would you use, and which variable would you integrate
              against? </p>
          </statement>

          <hint>
            <p> The curve <m>y=(2-x)(4-x)</m> is already given as a function of <m>x</m>. </p>
          </hint>

          </exercise>
      </page>

      <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in" xml:id="ex-volume-strategy-cylinders">
          <statement>
            <p> Two cylinders, each with radius <m>r</m>, have axes that meet at right angles. Find
              the volume common to both cylinders. </p>
          </statement>

          </exercise>
      </page>

      <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in" xml:id="ex-volume-strategy-both-ways">
          <introduction>
            <p> Let <m>R</m> be the region in the first quadrant bounded by the curves <m>y=x^3</m>
              and <m>y=2x-x^2</m>. </p>
          </introduction>

          <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
              <p> Find the volume of the solid obtained by revolving <m>R</m> around the <m>x</m>-axis.
              </p>
            </statement>

            </task>

          <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
              <p> Find the volume of the solid obtained by revolving <m>R</m> around the <m>y</m>-axis.
              </p>
            </statement>

            </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in" xml:id="ex-volume-strategy-choose-wisely">
          <introduction>
            <p> Let <m>R</m> be the region bounded on the right by <m>x=4</m> and on the left by <m>
                x=y^2</m>, for <m>-2\leq y\leq 2</m>. Notice that both boundaries are naturally
              functions of <m>y</m> — so no matter which axis we revolve around, we should
              integrate <m>dy</m>. Choose your method wisely. </p>
          </introduction>

          <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
              <p> Find the volume of the solid obtained by revolving <m>R</m> around the <m>x</m>-axis.
              </p>
            </statement>

            </task>

          <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
              <p> Find the volume of the solid obtained by revolving <m>R</m> around the <m>y</m>-axis.
              </p>
            </statement>

            </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <page>
        <exercise workspace="2in" xml:id="ex-volume-strategy-torus">
          <statement>
            <p> A solid torus (donut) is formed by revolving a disk of radius <m>b</m> centered at <m>
                (a,0)</m>, with <m>a &gt; b &gt; 0</m>, around the <m>y</m>-axis. Explain why shells
              (rather than washers) are the natural method here, and use them to find the volume of
              the torus. </p>
          </statement>

          </exercise>
      </page>
    </worksheet>
  </article>
</pretext>